.TH "READABLESTREAM.FROM_STATIC" "3JS" "July 4, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
ReadableStream.from_static \- ReadableStream: from() static method
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]Note:\f[R] This feature is available in Web Workers.
.PP
The \f[B]\f[CB]ReadableStream.from()\f[B]\f[R] static method returns a
\f[CR]ReadableStream\f[R] from a provided iterable or async iterable
object.
.PP
The method can be used to wrap iterable and async iterable objects as
readable streams, including arrays, sets, arrays of promises, async
generators, \f[CR]ReadableStreams\f[R], Node.js \f[CR]readable\f[R]
streams, and so on.
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
ReadableStream.from(anyIterable)
.EE
.SS Parameters
.TP
\f[B]anyIterable\f[R]
An iterable or async iterable object.
.SS Return value
A \f[CR]ReadableStream\f[R].
.SS Exceptions
.TP
\f[B]TypeError\f[R]
Thrown if the passed parameter is not an iterable or async iterable
(does not define the \f[CR][Symbol.iterator]()\f[R] or
\f[CR][Symbol.asyncIterator]()\f[R] method).
Also thrown if, during iteration, the result of the next step is not an
object or is a promise that does not resolve to an object.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Convert an async iterator to a ReadableStream
This live example demonstrates how you can convert an async iterable to
a \f[CR]ReadableStream\f[R], and then how this stream might be consumed.
.SS HTML
The HTML is consists of single \f[CR]<pre>\f[R] element, which is used
for logging.
.IP
.EX
<\f[B]pre\f[R] id=\(dqlog\(dq></\f[B]pre\f[R]>
.EE
.SS JavaScript
The example code creates a \f[CR]log()\f[R] function to write to the log
HTML element.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] logElement = document.getElementById(\(dqlog\(dq);
\f[B]function\f[R] log(text) {
  logElement.innerText += \(ga${text}\(rsn\(ga;
}
.EE
.PP
It then checks if the static method is supported, and if not, logs the
result.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]if\f[R] (!ReadableStream.from) {
  log(\(dqReadableStream.from() is not supported\(dq);
}
.EE
.PP
The async iterable is an anonymous generator function that yields the
values of 1, 2 and 3 when it is called three times.
This is passed to \f[CR]ReadableStream.from()\f[R] to create the
\f[CR]ReadableStream\f[R].
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// Define an asynchronous iterator\f[R]
\f[B]const\f[R] asyncIterator = (\f[B]async\f[R] \f[B]function\f[R]* () {
  \f[B]yield\f[R] 1;
  \f[B]yield\f[R] 2;
  \f[B]yield\f[R] 3;
})();

\f[I]// Create ReadableStream from iterator\f[R]
\f[B]const\f[R] myReadableStream = ReadableStream.from(asyncIterator);
.EE
.PP
Using readable streams demonstrates several ways to consume a stream.
The code below uses a \f[CR]for ...await\f[R] loop, as this method is
the simplest.
Each iteration of the loop logs the current chunk from the stream.
.IP
.EX
consumeStream(myReadableStream);

\f[I]// Iterate a ReadableStream asynchronously\f[R]
\f[B]async\f[R] \f[B]function\f[R] consumeStream(readableStream) {
  \f[B]for\f[R] \f[B]await\f[R] (\f[B]const\f[R] chunk \f[B]of\f[R] readableStream) {
    \f[I]// Do something with each chunk\f[R]
    \f[I]// Here we just log the values\f[R]
    log(\(gachunk: ${chunk}\(ga);
  }
}
.EE
.SS Result
The output of consuming the stream is shown below (if
\f[CR]ReadableStream.from()\f[R] is supported).
.SS Convert an Array to a ReadableStream
This example demonstrates how you can convert an \f[CR]Array\f[R] to a
\f[CR]ReadableStream\f[R].
.SS JavaScript
The iterable is just an array of strings that is passed to
\f[CR]ReadableStream.from()\f[R] to create the
\f[CR]ReadableStream\f[R].
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// An Array of vegetable names\f[R]
\f[B]const\f[R] vegetables = [\(dqCarrot\(dq, \(dqBroccoli\(dq, \(dqTomato\(dq, \(dqSpinach\(dq];

\f[I]// Create ReadableStream from the Array\f[R]
\f[B]const\f[R] myReadableStream = ReadableStream.from(vegetables);
.EE
.PP
We use the same approach as in the previous example log and to consume
the stream, so that is not shown here.
.SS Result
The output is shown below.
